---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { pageTitle, canonicalUrl, graphJsonLd, breadcrumbGraphNode, webPageGraphNode } from '../lib/seo';

const canonicalPath = '/editorial-policy/';
const title = pageTitle('Editorial Policy & Listing Standards');
const description = 'Where exam-prep community listings come from, the role of AI, exam classification, and how corrections and removals work.';
const pageUrl = canonicalUrl(canonicalPath);
const jsonLd = graphJsonLd([
  breadcrumbGraphNode([{ name: 'Editorial Policy', path: canonicalPath }], pageUrl),
  webPageGraphNode({ name: title, description, canonicalPath }),
]);
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonicalPath={canonicalPath}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-3xl px-4 py-10 sm:px-6">
    <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 sm:text-3xl">Editorial Policy</h1>

    <div class="prose-static mt-6">
      <h2>What is StudyGroupsHub's directory scope?</h2>
      <p>
        StudyGroupsHub is a directory of public exam-prep and professional-certification study
        communities on <a href="/platform/discord/">Discord</a>,{' '}
        <a href="/platform/telegram/">Telegram</a>, and WhatsApp. Listings are organized by exam and
        certification (<a href="/exam/sat/">SAT</a>, <a href="/exam/ielts/">IELTS</a>,{' '}
        <a href="/exam/usmle/">USMLE</a>, <a href="/exam/lsat/">LSAT</a>,{' '}
        <a href="/exam/cpa/">CPA</a>, <a href="/exam/cissp/">CISSP</a>,{' '}
        <a href="/exam/pmp/">PMP</a>, and others), exam family, target market, and study type.
        Every listing is a pointer to an external community; inclusion reflects public discovery,
        not endorsement.
      </p>

      <h2>Where listings come from</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Public discovery:</strong> Automated discovery searches publicly
          indexed sources for exam-focused community invite URLs. Only public information
          is used. We never join groups, scrape member data, or read private content.</li>
        <li><strong>Submissions:</strong> Anyone can submit a public study community via
          the submit form. Submissions are reviewed manually before publication.</li>
        <li><strong>Manual curation:</strong> Maintainers may add listings directly with
          documented public sources.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The role of AI and search providers</h2>
      <p>
        AI models and rule-based classifiers help generate discovery queries, classify candidate
        listings, suggest exams and families, and summarize public evidence. Search providers
        surface candidate pages. Neither AI nor a search provider establishes truth on its own;{' '}
        <strong>automated output is treated as a hypothesis, not a fact</strong>.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Real URLs are required:</strong> A listing is created only when a real,
          publicly reachable invite URL exists and is recorded. Automation never invents URLs,
          member counts, exam claims, or descriptions.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>AI never decides publication:</strong> New discoveries go to a pending
          queue for review before entering the public dataset.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>AI never scores or rates:</strong> It does not rank communities, compute
          "trust scores", or generate reviews.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Platform validation</h2>
      <p>
        Each listing records the platform the community actually uses. Where a platform offers
        a public API to confirm a community exists, such as Discord's public invite API returning
        a guild ID, we record it. Guild or group IDs are stored only from real API responses, never
        inferred, so we can deduplicate listings that share one community across rotating invite codes.
      </p>

      <h2>Exam classification</h2>
      <p>
        Listings are tagged with exams and certification bodies (such as{' '}
        <a href="/exam/nclex/">NCLEX</a>, <a href="/exam/cfa/">CFA</a>,{' '}
        <a href="/exam/cpa/">CPA</a>, <a href="/exam/cissp/">CISSP</a>, and{' '}
        <a href="/exam/pmp/">PMP</a>) only when the community's own public materials
        support the claim through its title, description, pinned content, or official links.
        Classifications follow the configured exam registry and exam-family taxonomy:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>An exam tag requires evidence; we never guess from a name alone.</li>
        <li>Families (College Admissions, English Proficiency, Medical &amp; Healthcare,
          Project Management, and others) are assigned from the community's primary exam focus.</li>
        <li>Target markets (US, UK, CA, AU, NZ, IE, Global English) are recorded only with
          evidence or config-justified defaults, never assumed.</li>
        <li>Communities without a single exam focus are held under General Study
          Communities rather than being force-tagged.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Source verification</h2>
      <p>
        Every listing stores its source URLs and verification state. Factual fields such as
        member counts, check dates, certification providers, and study types require real sources
        when possible. If a field cannot be confirmed, it is left unknown or empty rather
        than invented. See <a href="/how-we-verify/">How We Verify</a> for the exact
        meaning of each status.
      </p>

      <h2>Risk filtering</h2>
      <p>
        Candidates are screened for signs of harm before publication:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Exam leaks and dumps:</strong> Communities that openly trade leaked
          exam content or "verified dumps" are rejected. Promotion of stolen exam
          material is disqualifying.</li>
        <li><strong>Credential fraud:</strong> Proxy-testing services, score-fixing offers,
          and certificate mills are excluded.</li>
        <li><strong>Scam indicators:</strong> Pay-to-join schemes, unverifiable "guaranteed
          pass" claims, and impersonation of exam bodies are treated as red flags.</li>
        <li>
          Listings may carry internal <code>safetyFlags</code> (such as
          <code>potential-risk-language</code>) that guide review without us declaring the
          community a scam.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Passing this filter means no disqualifying risk was detected in public metadata; it is
        not a safety certification.
      </p>

      <h2>Manual moderation</h2>
      <p>
        Listings are reviewed against public evidence before publication: the invite URL is
        checked, the platform is confirmed, exam claims are matched to public metadata, and
        any description reflects factual evidence. Moderation decisions follow the rules in this
        policy, independent of paid or promotional interests.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        Corrections are accepted via the <a href="/report/">report form</a> or{' '}
        <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>. We review submissions against public evidence and
        update listings when justified.
      </p>

      <h2>Removal requests</h2>
      <p>
        Listings can be removed for confirmed illegality, clear fraud, exam-content trading,
        spam, or at the verified request of a community owner (<code>owner-confirmed</code>).
        Removal requests are handled by a human editor. We prefer marking listings unavailable
        over silently deleting indexed URLs so dead or removed listings retain an explanatory note.
      </p>

      <h2>Dead-link handling</h2>
      <p>
        Repeated strong failures (such as persistent 404 or 410 responses) move a link to{' '}
        <code>dead</code>. We do not mark a listing dead after one transient timeout, and
        bot-blocking or region blocks are never treated as evidence of a dead link.
      </p>

      <h2>Content copying</h2>
      <p>
        We do not republish full third-party content. Descriptions are concise, factual
        summaries based on public evidence. Exam names and certification trademarks belong
        to their respective owners and are used only to identify the focus of a community.
      </p>

      <h2>Advertising and sponsors</h2>
      <p>
        If advertising or sponsored listings are introduced later, they will be clearly
        labeled and never prioritized by payment in the organic listings.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
